From: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] x86/mm: flush TLB on permission upgrade in
 ptep_set_access_flags

When attempting to write to a CoW'd page, wp_page_reuse adds the
write permission via ptep_set_access_flags. Intel SDM Volume 3A
subsection 5.10.4.1 states that #PF does invalidate the TLB entry.
Assuming the SDM and Ice Lake are both correct, something else
non-deterministically re-populates the TLB with this read-only
page before the call to set_pte makes the pte writable. This
causes a spurious fault causing more instructions to be executed
which eventually affects the timestamp and eventually cascades
to other fields. Fix this by flushing the TLB after updating the
permission.

Signed-off-by: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
---
 arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c b/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
--- a/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c
@@ -394,8 +394,10 @@
 {
 	int changed = !pte_same(*ptep, entry);
 
-	if (changed && dirty)
+	if (changed && dirty) {
 		set_pte(ptep, entry);
+		flush_tlb_page(vma, address);
+	}
 
 	return changed;
 }
